                <pb facs="0460" n="316" sameAs="frus:frus1929v02/pg316"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">861.77 Chinese Eastern/311: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">MacMurray</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peiping</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1929-09-09T21:00:00+08:00">September 9, 1929—9
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received September 9—2:40 p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>808. Referring to my 805, Sept. 9, 5 p.m. Following from American Consul at
                    Harbin: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“September 9, noon. General Manager Fan confirms Customs and Japanese
                            official reports that railway station Suifenho destroyed by bombs from
                            Soviet aeroplanes. Casualties estimated at from 30 to 100. Town in
                            panic, railway staff escaped to Hsiaosuifen and Customs staff to
                            Muling.</p>
                        <p>Japanese residents could not leave on account of lack of rolling stock.
                            Firing near city heard until yesterday noon. Fan states that some
                            aeroplanes flying over Chalainor dropped few bombs but situation there
                            is not so serious as at Suifenho. States that night of September 7th
                            passenger train blown up near Suifenho, two railway employees killed,
                            others and some passengers wounded.”</p>
                    </quote></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">MacMurray</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
